/*
This example shows how to create property tests with contracts in order to verify method implementations under different scenarios. The code is organized by method and covers 5 properties: Count, IndexOf, LastIndexOf, Contains, and the return value of the Insert, Set, Add, and Get methods. 

Note that the example covers Add, Remove, Insert, Set and Get method tests and that a contract for each method is described and verified. Also we should write assert to ensure properties hold for all the possible paths of the method.
For each property, the old and new values for the property are captured and presented to the Pex framework for viewing in the output.
 
This is a typical approach when using Pex to create property tests with contracts based on method specifications. Here, the idea is to verify the implementation under different scenarios and conditions.


In the end, please note that Pex requires a runtime installed and Pex process for your .NET code.
It can handle many parameters and multiple branches. In the properties contracts, Pex assumes all elements are valid (i.e., the inputs meet the precondition). Thus for each parameter pair in the contract, Pex uses this assumption. For each edge case, Pex verifies the postcondition for 30 seeds and reports the results in PexReport.xml. 

I hope this helps in understanding how to write test with contracts in .pdb files or .NET. 

Please let me know if you are interested to explore more or to have a more detailed explanation.
*/
