A: This is a partial implementation of a proof-of-concept for a C#-based ArrayList-like API using the PexAPIWrapper methods used for Pex (a product by Microsoft). 

This code represents the contracts (specifications or requirements) of an ArrayList's operations. It aims to test these contracts, ensuring that Add, Remove, Insert, Get and Set operations of the ArrayList satisfy them in a certain range of input scenarios.

Please note, I made assumptions here as we didn’t have the actual ArrayList class. The classes and methods used (under the contract test class) are just placeholders.

The actual scenario may look different based on the actual context and requirements. In fact, it is a way to demonstrate how contract testing can be implemented for a hypothetical ArrayList API.

Each of the test methods represents a contract for a specific operation of the ArrayList (Add, Remove, Insert, Get and Set). In each contract method:
- old values of the list (before the operation) and new values (after) are obtained. 
- The pre/post conditions of the respective methods are verified against the actual values.

The actual contracts are represented in the comments above the methods (@PexMethod).

The method calls to PexObserve.ValueForViewing are for illustration purposes. In the final test run, they won't present any actionable output. They are used to provide some pre-processing output (before running the operation and after running the operation) for the observed values that can be seen in the test run's output.

Assertion is used to check the result (contains the element that we have inserted or not). If the element doesn't contain, it throws an exception saying "Assertion failed".

Unfortunately, Pex/Coverage/API profiler is not available in this environment and hence we are unable to execute this code here. However, you may perform the following steps on your local machine to execute this code:
1. Open Visual Studio.
2. Go to file -> New -> Project -> Class Library (Class Library (.NET Framework)).
3. Rename project to whatever you like e.g., "ArrayListContractTest"
4. Delete any auto generated code in the project and replace it with this code.
5. Go to Test -> Run All Tests.
6. Watch the result of each test in the test explorer which will be shown on the right panel.
7. You can also run code coverage, API profiling by clicking on the Test menu and choosing the corresponding option. 

This should help you to understand how you can design and execute a contract test on an ArrayList-like API. 

